==========================================
DAY 24 NOTES — Symbolic & Hard Links
Date: 22-09-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- Symlink (ln -s) = a pointer to a PATH, like a shortcut — breaks if the original is deleted
- Hard link (ln, no -s) = another name for the SAME DATA — survives original deletion
- ls -la shows symlinks as "link -> target" with an 'l' at the start of permissions
- Hard links look like normal files but have a link count > 1 in ls -la's 2nd column
- readlink -f shows the full absolute path a symlink resolves to
- Symlinks can cross filesystems and link to directories; hard links CANNOT do either
- Data behind a hard link only truly disappears once ALL hard links to it are removed

COMMANDS I PRACTICED:
[ ] ln -s (creating a symlink)
[ ] ln (creating a hard link)
[ ] ls -la (spotting link type and count)
[ ] readlink -f
[ ] testing deletion behavior for both link types

fill this in after running commands yourself :
$ ln -s original.txt mylink.txt && ls -la
> 

$ rm original.txt && cat mylink.txt (should be broken)
> 

$ ln realfile.txt hardlink.txt && rm realfile.txt && cat hardlink.txt (should still work)
> 


